          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            There are two kinds of higher dimensional generalizations of ordinary 3-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            that are often called “higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory” in the literature. Both are special cases of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            Recall that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            (not necessarily abelian) with non-generate binary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,-\rangle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            is ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            in dimension 3.
          %p
            But also every other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle-,-,\cdots,-\rangle
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            induces an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            Moreover, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+Lie+n-algebra"} line Lie n-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi b
                    %mi n
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b^n \mathbb{R}
            carries a canonical invariant polynomial. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
            associated with that is often called
            %em abelian higher dimensional CS theory
            \.
          %p (…)
          %p
            More general “higher”-generalization of Chern-Simons theory to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
            allow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            to be a (nonabelian)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+2-algebra"} Lie 2-algebra
            or more generally a (nonabelian)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"} L-infinity algebra
            or fully generally a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebroid"} L-infinity algebroid
          %h2#higher_abelian_chernsimons_theory Higher abelian Chern-Simons theory.
          %h3#definition Definition
          %p
            The definition of higher abelian Chern-Simons theory is simple
            %em locally
            when certain global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomological
            effects can be ignored. We first give the simple local definition and then the full global definition.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 4 k + 3
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
          %p
            Then the
            %em simple version
            of abelian
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                of degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2k+1
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Conf
                        %mi simpl
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi k
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      Conf_{simpl} = \Omega^{2k+1}(\Sigma)
                      \\,,
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
                is
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi dR
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      L : B \mapsto B \wedge d_{dR} B
                      \\,,
            %li
              %p
                and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi k
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      S : \Omega^{2k+1}(\Sigma) \to \mathbb{R}
              %p is therefore
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo ↦
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi Σ
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∧
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi dR
                      %mi B
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      S : B \mapsto \int_\Sigma B \wedge d_{dR}B
                      \\,.
          %p
            Notice that generally for an
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            on a closed
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_\Sigma B \wedge d_{dR} B
                  \=
                  (-1)^{1+n + n(1+n)}
                  \\int_\Sigma B \wedge d_{dR} B
          %p
            by first using integration by parts and then switching the order of the wedge factors. Therefore this kind of action vanishes identically when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi deg
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} deg B
            is even. This is the reason for the above assumption that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi deg
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} deg B = 2k+1
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            and hence that the Chern-Simons theory is in dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+3
          %p In the full theory instead the configuration space is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mi diff
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Conf = \mathbf{H}_{diff}^{2k+2}(\Sigma)
                  \\,,
          %p
            the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle (2k+1)-bundles with connection
            (given by
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2k+2
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            = succeed "-connections" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2k+1)
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2k+1)
          %p The action functional is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mover
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mover
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∪
                  %mover
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S : \hat B \mapsto \int_\Sigma \hat B \cup \hat B
                  \\,,
          %p
            where now the integral is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration+in+ordinary+differential+cohomology"} fiber integration in ordinary differential cohomology
            and in the integrand we have the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product+in+ordinary+differential+cohomology"} cup product in ordinary differential cohomology
            of differential cocycles.
          %p
            (See for instance (
            %a{:href => "#GT"}> GT, section 4.1
            ), (
            %a{:href => "#FMS"}> FMS, (1.28)
            )).
          %h3#formulation_in_chernsimons_theory
            Formulation in
            = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            We discuss how the above definition arises as a special case of the general notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
          %p These theories are defined by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebroid"} L-infinity algebroid
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            %li
              %p
                equipped with an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \rangle
          %p
            The abelian higher dimensional Chern-Simons theories in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+3
            are the special case of this general situation where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔞
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi b
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi k
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a} = b^{2k+1}\mathbb{R}
                is the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+Lie+n-algebra"} line Lie (2k+1)-algebra
                = succeed "-fold" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2k+1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
                of the abelian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle - \rangle
                is the canonical quadratic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
                on this.
          %p (…)
          %p
            See (
            %a{:href => "#FRS"}> FRS, 4.1.4
            ).
          %h3#examples Examples
          %p
            Higher dimensional abelian Chern-Simons theories appear automatically as components of systems of higher
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            (they are automatically induced by the requirement of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+supersymmetry"} local supersymmetry
            in these higher dimensional supergravity theories).
          %ul
            %li
              see at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
              the section
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/M5-brane#7dCSDual"} Conformal blocks and 7d Chern-Simons dual
          %h3#properties Properties
          %h4#holographic_relation_to_dimensional_theory
            Holographic relation to
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+2
          %p
            Higher Chern-Simons theory in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+3
            is related by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
            in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+2
            (at least in the abelian case).
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k=0)
                \: ordinary 3-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                is related to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
                on its boundary;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k=1)
                \: 7-dimensional Chern-Simons theory is related to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane"} fivebrane
                model on its boundary;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k=2)
                \: 11-dimensional Chern-Simons theory is related to a parts of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
                on its bounday (or that of the space-filling 9-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"}> brane
                , if one wishes) (
                %a{:href => "#BelovMoore"}> BelovMoore
                )
          %h4#BackgroundCharge Background charges and square root action functionals
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            is an example of a general phenomenon of higher abelian Chern-Simons QFTs in the presence of
            = succeed "." do
              %em background charge
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Witten96"} Witten
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HopkinsSinger05"} HopkinsSinger 05
            = succeed "-field" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            – see there for a discussion of this example. Here we discuss this effect generally, for higher abelian Chern-Simons theory in arbitrary dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+3
          %p
            Fix some natural number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            and an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+with+boundary"} with boundary
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4 k + 3
            = succeed "-form" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2k+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat G
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is an element in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat H^{2k+2}(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∪
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat G \cup \hat G \in \hat H^{4k+4}(X)
            of this with itself has a natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+holonomy"} higher holonomy
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(i S (-)) : \hat H^{2k+2}(X) \to U(1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∪
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hat G \mapsto \exp(i \int_X \hat G \cup \hat G)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the exponentiated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for bare
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 4
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (4k+3)
          %p
            Observe now that the above action functional may be regarded as a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+form"} quadratic form
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat H^{2k+2}(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+form"} bilinear form
            is the (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/secondary+characteristic+class"}> secondary
            ”, since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+3
            instead of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4k+4
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+pairing"} intersection pairing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle -,-\rangle : \hat H^{2k+2}(X) \times \hat H^{2k+2}(X) \to U(1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∪
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\hat a_1 , \hat a_2) \mapsto \exp(i \int_X \hat a_1 \cup \hat a_2 )
                  \\,.
          %p
            But note that from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S(-))
            we do
            %em not
            obtain a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+refinement"} quadratic refinement
            = succeed "of" do
              %br/
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  q : \hat H^{2k+2}(X) \to U(1)
          %p
            (not necessarily homogenous of degree 2 as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S(-))
            is), such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+pairing"} intersection pairing
            is reobtained from it by the polarization formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle \hat a_1, \hat a_2\rangle
                  \=
                  q(\hat a_1 + \hat a_2)
                  q(\hat a_1)^{-1}
                  q(\hat a_2)^{-1}
                  q(0)
                  \\,.
          %p
            If we took
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q := \exp(i S(-))
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,-\rangle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,-\rangle^2
            %em twice
            the integral.
          %p
            The observation in (
            %a{:href => "#Witten96"}> Witten96
            ) was that for the correct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic
            physics, we need instead an action functional which is indeed a genuine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+refinement"} quadratic refinement
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+pairing"} intersection pairing
          %p
            But since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential classes
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat H^{2k+2}(X)
            refine
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∪
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp( i \int_X \hat G \cup \hat G)
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi X
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo ∪
                    %mover
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp( i  \int_X \frac{1}{2} \hat G \cup \hat G)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariant
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+space"} simply connected
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %em levels
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \cup G
          %p
            Therefore the only way to obtain a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+root"} square root
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+form"} quadratic form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S(-))
            is to
            = succeed "." do
              %em shift its origin
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q : x \mapsto x^2
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            (a parabola in the plane). Replacing this by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mi λ
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q^{\lambda} : x \mapsto x^2 + \lambda x
            for some real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            means keeping the shape of the form, but shifting its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimum"} minimum
            from 0 to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\frac{1}{2}\lambda
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            previously with rotation-symmetric dynamics about
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = 0
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spontaneous+symmetry+breaking"} spontaneous symmetry breaking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = -\frac{1}{2}\lambda
            (and has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 4
                  %msup
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\frac{1}{4}\lambda^2
            there). We may say that there is a
            %em background field
            or
            %em
              background
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge"} charge
            that pushes the field out of its free equilibrium.
          %p
            To lift this reasoning to our action quadratic form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S(-))
            on differential cocycles, we need a differential class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \lambda \in H^{2k+2}(X)
            such that for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat a \in H^{2k+2}(X)
            the composite class
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∪
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo +
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∪
                  %mover
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hat a \cup \hat a + \hat a \cup \hat \lambda
                  \\in
                  H^{4k+4}(X)
          %p is even, hence is divisible by 2. Because then we could define a shifted action functional
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∪
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo +
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∪
                  %mover
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(i S^\lambda(-)) : \hat a \mapsto \exp(i \int_X \frac{1}{2}(\hat a \cup \hat a + \hat a \cup \hat \lambda))
                  \\,,
          %p
            where now the fraction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{2}
            in the integrand does make sense. One directly sees that if this exists, then this shifted action is indeed now a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+refinement"} quadratic refinement
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+pairing"} intersection pairing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo +
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∪
                  %mover
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(i S^\lambda(\hat a + \hat b))
                  \\exp(i S^\lambda(\hat a))^{-1}
                  \\exp(i S^\lambda(\hat b))^{-1}
                  \\exp(i S^\lambda(0)
                  \=
                  \\exp(i \int_X \hat a \cup \hat b))
                  \\,.
          %p
            The condition on the existence of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat \lambda
            here means equivalently that the image of the underlying integral class in cohomology with coefficients in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            vanishes:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mn 4
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (a)_{\mathbb{Z}_2} \cup (a)_{\mathbb{Z}_2}
                  +
                  (a)_{\mathbb{Z}_2} \cup (\lambda)_{\mathbb{Z}_2}
                  \=
                  0 \in H^{4k+4}(X, \mathbb{Z}_2)
                  \\,,
          %p
            Precisely such a class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda)_{\mathbb{Z}_2}
            does uniquely exist on every oriented manifold. It is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wu+class"} Wu class
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ν
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu_{2k+2} \in H^{2k+2}(X,\mathbb{Z}_2)
            %em defined
            by this condition. Moreover, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} Spin-manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wu+class"} Wu class
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ν
                      %mrow
                        %mn 4
                        %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu_{4k}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            does exist as required above
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ν
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\lambda)_{\mathbb{Z}_2}
                  \=
                  \\nu_{2k+2}
                  \\,.
          %p
            It is given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomials"} polynomials
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontryagin+classes"} Pontryagin classes
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+fractional+Pontryagin+class"} first fractional Pontryagin class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{2}p_1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ν
                    %mn 4
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\frac{1}{2}p_1)_{\mathbb{Z}_2} = \nu_4
                  \\,.
          %p
            This was the original observation in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Witten96"} Witten96, around (3.3)
          %p
            Notice that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of the shifted action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S^\lambda(\hat a))
            are no longer
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_a = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_a = - \frac{1}{2}F_\lambda
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell+equations"} Maxwell equations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mover
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\frac{1}{2}\hat \lambda
            here plays the role of a
            %em
              background
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge"} charge
            (or rather, of the
            %em
              background
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current"} current
            that underlies a background charge). We therefore think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S^\lambda(-))
            as the exponentiated action functional for
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                higher dimensional abelian Chern-Simons theory with background charge
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 2
                      %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\frac{1}{2}\lambda
          %p
            This of course only makes sense if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            is further divisible by 2, which we will assume now. In (
            %a{:href => "#HopkinsSinger05"}> Hopkins-Singer 05
            ) is discussed a way to make sense of the further division in general if one passes to a certain notion of twisted differential cohomology. One can also adopt a different perspective and interpret the condition that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{2}p_1
            is further divisible by 2 precisely as a
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} String
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{String}^{2a}
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Twisted+Differential+String+and+Fivebrane+Structures"} SSS3
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin%5Ec+structure"} Spin^c structure
          %p With respect to the shifted action functional it makes sense to introduce the shifted field
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mi λ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hat G  := \hat a  + \frac{1}{2}\lambda
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is simply a re-parameterization such that the Chern-Simons equations of motion again look homogenous, namely
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FieldStrength
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FieldStrength(\hat G) = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(i S^\lambda(\hat a))
            from above equivalently reads
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo ∪
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mover
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(i S^\lambda(\hat G)) =
                  \\exp(
                  i \int_X \frac{1}{2}(\hat G \cup \hat G - (\frac{1}{2}\hat \lambda)^2)
                  )
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the form of the action functional first given as (
            %a{:href => "#Witten96"}> Witten96 (3.6)
            ) in for the case
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 1
          %p
            In the language of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+differential+c-structures"} twisted differential c-structures
          %p
            in order for the action functional of higher abelian Chern-Simons theory to be correctly divisible, the images of the fields in
            = succeed "-cohomology" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+Wu+structure"} twisted Wu structure
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                twisted
                = succeed "-structure" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 1
            this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+differential+string+structure"} twisted string structure
            and explains for instance the quantization condition on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
          %h2#higherdimensional_nonabelian_cs_theory Higher-dimensional non-abelian CS theory
          %p
            Chern-Simons actions for
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            but with higher-degree
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝔰𝔦𝔰𝔬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{siso}
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincare+Lie+algebra"} super Poincare Lie algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+supergravity"} Chern-Simons supergravity
          %h3#1d_chernsimons_theory 1d Chern-Simons theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 1d Chern-Simons theory
          %h3#2d_chernsimons_theory 2d Chern-Simons theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 2d Chern-Simons theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
          %h3#3d_chernsimons_theory 3d Chern-Simons theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+model"} Dijkgraaf-Witten model
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Courant+sigma-model"} Courant sigma-model
          %h3#4d_chernsimons_theory 4d Chern-Simons theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 4d Chern-Simons theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yetter+model"} Yetter model
          %h3#5d_chernsimons_theory 5d Chern-Simons theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/5d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 5d Chern-Simons theory
          %h3#6d_chernsimons_theory 6d Chern-Simons theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 6d Chern-Simons theory
          %h3#7d_chernsimons_theory 7d Chern-Simons theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7d Chern-Simons theory
          %h3#11d_chernsimons_theory 11d Chern-Simons theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 11d Chern-Simons theory
          %h3#infinitedimensional_chernsimons_theory Infinite-dimensional Chern-Simons theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} infinite-dimensional Chern-Simons theory
          %h3#aksz_models
            AKSZ
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AKSZ+sigma-model"} AKSZ sigma-model
          %h3#string_field_theory String field theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wu+classes"} Wu classes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} infinity-Chern-Simons theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %strong higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+gravity"} Chern-Simons gravity
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AKSZ+sigma-model"} AKSZ sigma-model
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma+model"} Poisson sigma model
                      %ul
                        %li
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"}> A-model
                          ,
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Courant+sigma-model"} Courant sigma-model
                      %ul
                        %li
                          %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} 1-dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+WZW+models"} higher dimensional WZW models
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p For higher dimensional non-abelian Chern-Simons theory see for instance
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Máximo Bañados,
                %em Higher dimensional Chern-Simons theories and black holes
                (
                %a{:href => "http://worldscibooks.com/etextbook/4388/4388_chap01.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Máximo Bañados, Luis Garay,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"} Marc Henneaux
                %em Existence of local degrees of freedom for higher dimensional pure Chern-Simons theories
                Phys. Rev. D 53, R593–R596 (1996) (
                %a{:href => "http://prd.aps.org/pdf/PRD/v53/i2/pR593_1"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Máximo Bañados, Luis Garay,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"} Marc Henneaux
                %em The dynamical structure of higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9605159"}> arXiv:hep-th/9605159
                )
            %li
              %p
                G Giachetta, L Mangiarotti, G Sardanashvily,
                %em Noether conservation laws in higher-dimensional Chern-Simons theory
                Modern Physics Letters A Volume 18(2003) (
                %a{:href => "http://gnsardan.appfarm.ru/a84.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Danhua Song, Mengyao Wu, Ke Wu, Jie Yang,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higher Chern-Simons based on (2-)crossed modules
                %strong 2023
                207 (2023) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04667"}> arXiv:2212.04667
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/JHEP07(2023)207"} doi:10.1007/JHEP07(2023)207
          %h3#formulation_in_differential_cohomology Formulation in differential cohomology
          %p
            For the formulation of abelian Chern-Simons theory by fiber integration over cup products in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            see
          %ul
            %li
              %p#GT
                Enore Guadagnini, Frank Thuillier,
                %em Deligne-Beilinson cohomology and abelian links invariants
                SIGMA 4 (2008), 078, 30 (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0801.1445"}> arXiv:0801.1445
                )
            %li
              %p
                R. Floreanini, R. Percacci,
                %em Higher dimensional abelian Chern-Simons theory
                Physics Letters B Volume 224, Issue 3, 29 (1989)
            %li
              %p#FMS
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gregory+Moore"} Gregory Moore
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"} Graeme Segal
                %em The Uncertainty of Fluxes
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0605198"}> arXiv:hep-th/0605198
                )
          %h3#relation_to_selfdual_theories Relation to self-dual theories
          %p
            The idea of describing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
            by abelian Chern-Simons theory in one dimension higher originates in
          %ul#Witten96
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              %em Five-Brane Effective Action In M-Theory
              Journal of Geometry and Physics, Volume 22, Issue 2, May 1997, Pages 103-133 (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9610234"}> arXiv:hep-th/9610234
              )
          %p
            (there for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-susy+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-susy QFT
            on the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane"} fivebrane
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              %em Duality Relations Among Topological Effects In String Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9912086"}> arXiv:hep-th/9912086
              )
          %p
            Motivated by this the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            of self-dual fields had been discussed in
          %ul
            %li#HopkinsSinger05
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Hopkins"}> Mike Hopkins
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isadore+Singer"} Isadore Singer
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quadratic+Functions+in+Geometry%2C+Topology%2C+and+M-Theory"} Quadratic Functions in Geometry, Topology, and M-Theory
          %p More discussion of the general principle is in
          %ul
            %li
              Dmitriy Belov,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Moore"} Greg Moore
              %em Holographic Action for the Self-Dual Field
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0605038"}> arXiv:hep-th/0605038
              )
          %p
            The application of this to the description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            in 10-dimensions to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/infinity-Chern-Simons+theory"} 11-dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            is in the followup
          %ul#BelovMoore
            %li
              Dmitriy Belov,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Moore"} Greg Moore
              %em Type II Actions from 11-Dimensional Chern-Simons Theories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0611020"}> arXiv:hep-th/0611020
              )
          %h3#higher_chernsimons_supergravity Higher Chern-Simons (super)gravity
          %p
            There are various discussions identifying or conjecturing higher dimensional Chern-Simons theories as parts of or related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
          %p An original articles includes
          %ul
            %li
              Máximo Bañados, Ricardo Troncoso, Jorge Zanelli,
              %em Higher dimensional Chern-Simons supergravity
              Phys. Rev. D 54, 2605–2611 (1996) (
              %a{:href => "http://cdsweb.cern.ch/record/293524/files/9601003.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p An introduction and survey is in
          %ul
            %li
              Jorge Zanelli,
              %em Lecture notes on Chern-Simons (super-)gravities
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0502193"}> arXiv:hep-th/0502193
              )
          %p For more references see at
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+gravity"} Chern-Simons gravity
              \.
          %h3#ReferencesInvariants Higher Chern-Simons invariants
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                F. Thuillier,
                %em Deligne-Beilinson cohomology and abelian link invariants: torsion case
                J. Math. Phys. 50, 122301 (2009)
            %li
              %p
                B. Broda,
                %em Higher-dimensional Chern-Simons theory and link invariants
                Physics Letters B Volume 280, Issues 3-4, 30 (1992) Pages 213-218
            %li
              %p
                L. Gallot, E. Pilon, F. Thuillier,
                %em Higher dimensional abelian Chern-Simons theories and their link invariants
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1207.1270"}> arXiv:1207.1270
                )
          %h3#formulation_in_chernsimons_theory_2
            Formulation in
            = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Below (5.11) of (
            %a{:href => "#HopkinsSinger05"}> Hopkins-Singer 05
            ).
          %p Section 4.1.4 of
          %ul
            %li#FRS
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Domenico+Fiorenza"}> Domenico Fiorenza
              ,
              = succeed "," do
